5. The GitHub practice system

Every single thing you practise goes on GitHub. Not because a green contribution graph impresses
anyone by itself, but because it forces you to finish things, write clearly about them, and build a public
record that a recruiter can open in thirty seconds.

Repository name                                                  What goes inside

your_username (profile README)                                   A short introduction, your target role, tech stack, current focus, and links to
                                                                 your best projects and LinkedIn.

python_core_practice                                             Folders by topic: oop, exceptions, file_handling, decorators and so on, each
                                                                 with code and notes.md

dsa_in_python                                                    Folders by topic: arrays, strings, hashing, trees, graphs, dp. One file per
                                                                 problem with link, approach and complexity.

sql_practice                                                     One .sql file per topic plus your own database design project.

fastapi_learning                                                 Small numbered folders, one concept each, built while following the docs.

Project repositories                                             One separate repo for each of the five projects in Section 6, each with a proper
                                                                 README.

Use underscores in repository names, keep them lowercase, and keep names meaningful. A name like
test123 or my_project_final tells the reader nothing


Rules that make your GitHub look professional

1 Commit every day you study, even if it is one DSA solution. Never create fake commits or copy
someone else's repository. Interviewers do ask you to explain your code line by line.
2 Write clear commit messages that describe what changed, for example: "Add JWT login endpoint",
"Fix total calculation for monthly report", "Write tests for expense routes". Avoid messages like
"update" or "final".
3 Never push passwords, API keys or .env files. Add them to .gitignore and share a .env.example file
with empty values instead.
4 Every project repo must have a README with: project title and one line description, a screenshot or
GIF, the live link, features, tech stack, how to run it locally, API endpoint list, what you learned, and
future improvements.
5 Pin your best four to six repositories on your profile, with your strongest project first.
6 Once you are comfortable, use branches for new features and merge through pull requests, even
when working alone. It shows you understand real team workflow.
7 In Phase 3, add GitHub Actions to run your tests automatically on every push. It is a small effort and
very few freshers do it
